==English==

===Etymology===
{{etystub}}

The term is said to have originated on the early-American East Coast prior to modern day burial standards such as embalming. Medical understanding was limited at best, so on occasion patients were pronounced dead when they were actually in a "light" coma or under some other condition where they were unconscious and their heartbeat was lowered. To prevent being buried alive, graves were fashioned with large bells that sat above ground while a string or cord was attached at one end to the corpse and at the other end to the bell. The idea behind this was that if the buried person awoke while in the grave, they could simply ring the bell and wait to be dug up. (This is the source of the phrase "Saved by the bell.")

In cities like Savannah, GA and Charleston, SC, it was said that if someone were dug up and entered back into society after overcoming the condition that caused their pronounced death, their actions were sometimes odd and unlike the personalities they possessed prior to being buried alive. These oddly acting people were called "Dead Ringers," because while they looked the same, they were very different in personality and action.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|sg=[[dead]] [[ringer]]}}

# {{idiom}} Someone or something that very closely resembles another; someone or something easily mistaken for another.
#:''He is a '''dead ringer''' for his grandfather at that age.''

